---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "TRUE Treadmill Buyer’s Guide (2026): Models, Warranty, Dealers";
const description =
  "TRUE Fitness sells its home treadmills through dealers and its own store. The Z500 and Performance 1000, 3000 and 8000, the warranty fine print, and how TRUE compares.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "true-z500",
    name: "TRUE Z500",
    category: "Compact Non-Folding Home Treadmill",
    badge: "Lowest-Priced TRUE",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=non+folding+treadmill+22+inch+deck&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$3,499 at TRUE on 20 Oct 2026",
    priceBounds: {"low":3499,"high":3499},
    specs: "20 x 54 in. running surface · 3 HP (lab rating) · 0.5-12 mph · 0-15% incline · 350 lb max user · 8.5 in. LCD",
    pros: [
      "The cheapest way into a new TRUE treadmill",
      "Compact 72 by 27-inch footprint for a non-folding machine",
      "350 lb maximum user weight, the same as the Performance Series",
      "25-year frame and 15-year motor cover on TRUE’s residential terms",
    ],
    cons: [
      "A 54-inch running surface is short for running; it suits walking and jogging",
      "No heart-rate chest strap in the box, and no touchscreen option",
      "Parts cover is five years and labour one year",
      "Still about $3,500, where many brands sell a 60-inch deck for less",
    ],
    bottomLine: "A sensible TRUE for walkers and joggers short on floor space; anyone who runs at pace should step up to a 60-inch Performance model.",
    description: "The Z500 is TRUE’s compact home treadmill. TRUE’s product page and 2026 spec sheet, and The Fitness Outlet’s listing, agree on a 20 by 54-inch running surface, a 3 HP motor, 0.5 to 12 mph, 0 to 15% incline, a 350 lb maximum user weight, a 249 lb machine weight and a 72 by 27 by 57-inch footprint. It uses TRUE’s FITX deck cushioning and an 8.5-inch LCD console with contact heart-rate grips; TRUE says a Polar chest strap is not included and recommends one. TRUE footnotes its horsepower figures as laboratory dynamometer ratings rather than working horsepower at home. Both sources give the same residential warranty: 25 years on the frame, 15 on the motor, five on parts, five on the console and one year of labour. TRUE listed it at $3,499 on 20 October 2026; The Fitness Outlet showed a sale price of $3,199 the same day.",
  },
  {
    id: "true-performance-1000",
    name: "TRUE Performance 1000",
    category: "Entry Performance Series Treadmill",
    badge: "Entry 60-Inch Deck",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=non+folding+treadmill+22+inch+deck&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$5,199-$6,199 at TRUE on 20 Oct 2026, by console",
    priceBounds: {"low":5199,"high":6199},
    specs: "21 x 60 in. running surface · 3 HP (lab rating) · 0.5-12 mph · 0-15% incline · 350 lb max user · HRC with strap",
    pros: [
      "A 21 by 60-inch running surface on the cheapest Performance model",
      "HRC Cruise Control and a Polar heart-rate strap included",
      "25-year frame and 25-year motor cover",
      "Choice of 10, 16 or 22-inch touchscreen consoles at TRUE",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Sources disagree on parts cover: five years on TRUE’s page, seven on its 2025 spec sheet",
      "Standard belt rather than the orthopedic belt on the 3000 and 8000",
      "Price varies by more than $1,000 between sellers and consoles",
      "Top speed of 12 mph, like most home treadmills",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The TRUE most home runners should start with: the full 60-inch deck and heart-rate control without paying for features they may not use.",
    description: "The Performance 1000 is the entry model in TRUE’s Performance Series. TRUE’s product page, its 2025 spec sheet and The Fitness Outlet’s listing agree on a 21 by 60-inch running surface, a 3 HP motor (a laboratory rating, TRUE says), 0.5 to 12 mph, 0 to 15% incline, a 350 lb user rating, a 297 lb machine weight and a 79 by 33.5 by 53-inch footprint. It has FITX cushioning, a standard lubricant-infused belt, 3-inch rollers, contact grips and HRC Cruise Control with a Polar strap included. Frame and motor are covered for 25 years and labour for one. Parts cover is where the sources part company: TRUE’s current page and the retailer give five years, the 2025 spec sheet seven, and console cover is three years on the current page against five on the older sheet, which also listed different consoles. Get the terms for your configuration in writing. TRUE listed it at $5,199 to $6,199 on 20 October 2026; The Fitness Outlet showed $4,199, and we could not confirm which console that price includes.",
  },
  {
    id: "true-performance-3000",
    name: "TRUE Performance 3000",
    category: "Mid Performance Series Treadmill",
    badge: "Orthopedic Belt",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=non+folding+treadmill+22+inch+deck&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$5,499-$6,499 at TRUE on 20 Oct 2026, by console",
    priceBounds: {"low":5499,"high":6499},
    specs: "21 x 60 in. running surface · 3 HP (lab rating) · 0.5-12 mph · 0-15% incline · 350 lb max user · orthopedic belt",
    pros: [
      "Adds TRUE’s orthopedic belt to the Performance 1000’s frame and deck",
      "HRC Cruise Control with a Polar strap included",
      "Seven years of parts cover on TRUE’s page, longer than the 1000’s",
      "25-year frame and motor cover",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Same motor rating, speed, incline and user weight as the cheaper 1000",
      "One retailer’s comparison table gives five years of parts cover, not seven",
      "No Soft Select adjustable deck; that is reserved for the 8000",
      "One year of labour",
    ],
    bottomLine: "Worth the extra $300 over the 1000 at TRUE’s prices if you want the softer belt and longer parts cover; otherwise the 1000 does the same job.",
    description: "The Performance 3000 shares the Performance 1000’s frame. TRUE’s product page and The Fitness Outlet agree on a 21 by 60-inch running surface, a 3 HP motor, 0.5 to 12 mph, 0 to 15% incline, a 350 lb user rating, a 297 lb machine weight and a 79 by 33.5 by 53-inch footprint. The difference is the belt, which TRUE describes as a lubricant-infused orthopedic belt, used with the same FITX cushioning. HRC Cruise Control and a Polar strap come with it. TRUE gives 25 years on the frame and motor, seven on parts, three on the console and one of labour; The Fitness Outlet’s warranty section agrees on seven years of parts, though its own comparison table says five. TRUE listed it at $5,499 to $6,499 on 20 October 2026, and The Fitness Outlet at a sale price of $4,499.",
  },
  {
    id: "true-performance-8000",
    name: "TRUE Performance 8000",
    category: "Flagship Home Treadmill With Adjustable Deck",
    badge: "Soft Select Deck",
    amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=non+folding+treadmill+22+inch+deck&tag=sktiger-20",
    amazonLabel: "Shop Similar on Amazon",
    reviewUrl: null,
    priceRange: "$5,999-$6,999 at TRUE on 20 Oct 2026, by console",
    priceBounds: {"low":5999,"high":6999},
    specs: "60 in. long running surface · 3 HP (lab rating) · 0.5-12 mph · 0-15% incline · 350 lb max user · Soft Select deck",
    pros: [
      "Soft Select lets you set the deck from softer to firmer",
      "Ten years of parts cover, the longest in TRUE’s home range",
      "Reversible deck and orthopedic belt",
      "Zwift compatibility named on TRUE’s page and spec sheet",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Running-surface width is 22 inches on TRUE’s page but 21 inches on its 2025 sheet and Abt’s listing",
      "Same 3 HP rating, 12 mph top speed and 350 lb limit as the 1000",
      "Heaviest of the four at 330 lb",
      "One year of labour, as on every TRUE home treadmill",
    ],
    bottomLine: "The TRUE to buy if adjustable cushioning and the longest parts cover matter to you; it is not faster or stronger than the 1000.",
    description: "The Performance 8000 is TRUE’s top home treadmill. TRUE’s product page, its 2025 spec sheet and The Fitness Outlet agree on a 60-inch running length, a 3 HP motor, 0.5 to 12 mph, 0 to 15% incline, a 350 lb user rating, a 330 lb machine weight and a 79 by 33.5 by 53-inch footprint. What sets it apart is Soft Select, TRUE’s adjustable deck, which TRUE says ranges from soft like a track to firm like pavement, plus a reversible deck and an orthopedic belt. HRC Cruise Control and a heart-rate strap are included. Width is the unresolved figure: TRUE’s page and the retailer say 22 inches, while the 2025 spec sheet and Abt say 21 inches, so measure or ask. TRUE, Abt and The Fitness Outlet agree on 25 years for the frame and motor, ten on parts and one of labour. TRUE listed it at $5,999 to $6,999 on 20 October 2026, The Fitness Outlet at a sale price of $4,999, and Abt at $4,600 for the base without a console, by phone or in store only.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Brands", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/brands/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "TRUE Fitness Treadmills", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/true-fitness-treadmills/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Who makes TRUE treadmills?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "TRUE Fitness Technology, a privately held, family-owned company in the St. Louis area of Missouri, founded by Frank Trulaske. Its site gives its headquarters as St. Charles; older releases say O’Fallon. TRUE dates its start to 1979 as TRUE Sports, bought Octane Fitness in 2020, and is not part of iFIT, Johnson Health Tech or Dyaco.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does a TRUE treadmill cost?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "On 20 October 2026 TRUE’s store listed the Z500 at $3,499, the Performance 1000 at $5,199 to $6,199, the 3000 at $5,499 to $6,499 and the 8000 at $5,999 to $6,999, depending on console. A specialist dealer showed each model for $300 to $1,000 less the same day. Prices change, so compare quotes on the same console and delivery terms.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between the TRUE Performance 1000, 3000 and 8000?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They share a 60-inch running length, a 3 HP rating, 12 mph, 15% incline and a 350 lb limit. The 3000 adds an orthopedic belt and longer parts cover. The 8000 adds the Soft Select adjustable deck, a reversible deck and ten years of parts cover. None is more powerful than the others.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What warranty comes with a TRUE treadmill?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Current home terms are 25 years on the frame, 25 years on the motor for the Performance models and 15 for the Z500, five to ten years on parts, and one year of labour. TRUE says the warranty is valid only after first assembly by an authorised dealer or technician, must be registered within 30 days, and does not transfer.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is TRUE HRC?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "HRC is TRUE’s heart-rate control, which it patented in 1996. With HRC Cruise Control you set a target heart rate and the treadmill adjusts speed and incline to hold it. The Performance models include a Polar strap, which gives far steadier readings than hand grips. Ask your doctor what target to use if you have a heart condition.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I buy a TRUE treadmill on Amazon?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "We could not confirm a new TRUE home treadmill sold on Amazon when we checked. TRUE sells through its own store and authorised dealers, and buying from one matters, because TRUE’s warranty depends on authorised first assembly and dealers carry out warranty service.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", "@id": "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#shaheer-ali-khan", name: "Shaheer Ali Khan", honorificSuffix: "PhD", jobTitle: "Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist", url: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/editorial-standards/#who", sameAs: ["https://www.linkedin.com/in/shaheer-ali-khan-data/"], worksFor: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" } },
      datePublished: "2026-10-20",
      dateModified: "2026-10-20",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: product.name.split(" ")[0] },
      ...(product.priceBounds
        ? {
            offers: {
              "@type": "AggregateOffer",
              priceCurrency: "USD",
              lowPrice: product.priceBounds.low,
              highPrice: product.priceBounds.high,
              url: product.amazonUrl,
            },
          }
        : {}),
    })),
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: 4,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: `https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/true-fitness-treadmills/#${product.id}`,
      })),
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-10-20",
    modifiedTime: "2026-10-20",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "true treadmill",
      "true fitness treadmill",
      "true treadmills",
      "true performance 8000 treadmill",
      "true z500 treadmill",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "See the Current Range", link: "#lineup" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-truefitnesstreadmills" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-truefitnesstreadmills)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated October 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          TRUE Fitness Treadmills: <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">What to Buy, and What to Check First</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          TRUE is a family-owned Missouri maker better known in gyms than in living rooms. This guide covers who TRUE is, the four home treadmills it sells now, what its deck and heart-rate features do, how the warranty really reads, and how a TRUE compares with Sole, Spirit and Landice.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            The Short Answer
          </a>
          <a href="#lineup" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            The Current Range
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>


    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="48" height="48" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight"><a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author" class="text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a></p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">October 20, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="1.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-blue-50 border border-blue-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-700 m-0 leading-relaxed">
          <strong class="text-gray-900">What we checked, and what we have not done.</strong> On
          20 October 2026 we checked TRUE Fitness's commercial site (its Our Story page, cardio series
          pages, the Apex and Stryker Slat treadmill pages and its FAQ) and its home store at
          shop.truefitness.com (the treadmill collection, the Z500, Performance 1000, 3000 and 8000
          product pages, its warranty, FAQ and shipping pages and the 2026 Z500 spec sheet). We
          cross-checked the home models against The Fitness Outlet's listings, Abt's listing and the
          2025 TRUE spec sheets Abt hosts, the Apex against Schellers' listing, and the company's
          history against Treadmill Review Guru's TRUE coverage and 2020 trade-press reports of the
          Octane Fitness acquisition. We searched the US Consumer Product Safety Commission's recall
          database, which lists a 2023 TRUE console recall and a 1998 motor recall; both are covered
          below. Figures on product cards are ones on which TRUE and at least one independent source
          agree; where they disagree we say so rather than choose. We have not tested any TRUE
          treadmill. Prices are what each seller showed on the day we checked (20 Oct 2026) and they
          change. As an Amazon Associate we earn from qualifying purchases; TRUE home treadmills are
          sold mainly through TRUE and its dealers, so an Amazon button may not find the exact model.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>


    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <div class="not-prose bg-orange-50 border border-orange-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-3">Our Top Picks</h2>
        <ul class="list-none m-0 p-0">
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Lowest-Priced TRUE:</span>
            <a href="#true-z500" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">TRUE Z500</a>
            <a href="#true-z500" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Entry 60-Inch Deck:</span>
            <a href="#true-performance-1000" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">TRUE Performance 1000</a>
            <a href="#true-performance-1000" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Orthopedic Belt:</span>
            <a href="#true-performance-3000" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">TRUE Performance 3000</a>
            <a href="#true-performance-3000" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2 py-2 border-b border-orange-100 last:border-0">
            <span class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#FF5A1F]">Soft Select Deck:</span>
            <a href="#true-performance-8000" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">TRUE Performance 8000</a>
            <a href="#true-performance-8000" class="ml-auto text-xs font-bold text-[#0F62FE] no-underline hover:underline">Jump to review &darr;</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Are TRUE Treadmills Good? The Short Answer</a>
          <a href="#who-makes-true" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Who Makes TRUE Treadmills?</a>
          <a href="#lineup" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. The Current TRUE Home Treadmill Range</a>
          <a href="#which-model" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Z500 or Performance 1000, 3000 or 8000: Which One?</a>
          <a href="#deck-and-hrc" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Soft System, FITX, Soft Select and HRC Explained</a>
          <a href="#warranty" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. The TRUE Warranty, Read Properly</a>
          <a href="#where-to-buy" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Dealer-Only Buying: What It Means for Price and Service</a>
          <a href="#commercial" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. TRUE Commercial Treadmills, and Putting One at Home</a>
          <a href="#true-vs-rivals" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. TRUE vs Sole, Spirit and Landice</a>
          <a href="#used-true" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Buying a Used TRUE Treadmill</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">11. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people who search for a TRUE treadmill have used one already, usually in a hotel, a
          YMCA or an apartment-block gym, and want to know whether the home version is the same
          machine. The short version is that TRUE is a commercial-first company with a small home
          range, sold through specialist dealers and its own online store rather than big-box
          retailers. That makes it easy to overpay, easy to buy the wrong configuration, and easy to
          void the warranty by assembling the machine yourself. This guide sets out what TRUE sells for
          the home today, which figures the sources agree on and which they do not, how the
          dealer-and-installer model affects price and service, and how a TRUE stacks up against the
          brands it shares a showroom with.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Are TRUE Treadmills Good? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>A TRUE treadmill is a well-built, heavy, non-folding machine from a company that
            mainly makes gym equipment, and it is a good buy if you will pay dealer prices for long
            frame and motor cover, a 60-inch deck and heart-rate control that actually steers the
            workout.</strong> It is a poor buy if you want the most features per dollar, a folding
            frame, or a machine you can have dropped on the drive and assemble yourself.
          </p>
          <p>
            The current home range is four treadmills. The Z500 is the compact one, with a 54-inch
            running surface, at $3,499 at TRUE on the day we checked. The Performance 1000, 3000 and
            8000 share a 60-inch deck, a 3 HP motor rating, a 12 mph top speed, 15% incline and a
            350 lb user limit; they differ in the belt, the cushioning, the parts warranty and the
            price, which ran from $5,199 to $6,999 at TRUE depending on model and console. Specialist
            retailers were selling the same models for noticeably less on the same day.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three things decide whether a TRUE is right for you. First, the Performance models are not
            more powerful than one another; the 8000 buys you a better deck, not more motor. Second,
            TRUE's residential warranty is valid only if a TRUE dealer or authorised technician does
            the first assembly, unless TRUE agrees otherwise in writing. Third, prices vary by more
            than a thousand dollars between sellers for what looks like the same machine, so the
            console and delivery terms in a quote matter more than the headline figure. The cards
            below give the agreed figures for each model; the sections after them explain the rest.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
