      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="who-makes-true" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Who Makes TRUE Treadmills?</h2>
<p>
            TRUE makes them itself. TRUE Fitness Technology is a privately held company in the St.
            Louis area of Missouri. Its own site gives its headquarters as 1 Glazer Way, St. Charles,
            Missouri; earlier press releases and the CPSC's 2023 recall notice place it in nearby
            O'Fallon, and a 2020 release said its operations would remain in St. Louis. All three
            describe the same metro area, but if you see different towns on different pages, that is
            why.
          </p>
          <p>
            TRUE's Our Story page says the company started in 1979 as TRUE Sports with its first
            treadmill and became TRUE Fitness Technologies in 1981; a 2015 press release gave 1981 as
            the founding year, which is the renaming date on TRUE's own timeline. The founder is Frank
            Trulaske, still named as chief executive in 2020 coverage. TRUE calls itself the oldest
            family-owned and operated company in the industry and the largest privately held fitness
            equipment maker in the US. We cannot verify the second claim, but the private, family
            ownership is consistent across its site and trade coverage.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">TRUE, Octane and the commercial business</h3>
          <p>
            TRUE entered the commercial market in 1988, and that is still the larger part of what it
            does. In October 2020 it bought Octane Fitness, the elliptical maker, from Nautilus; TRUE's
            timeline and trade reports of the deal agree on that. Octane's products and support pages
            now sit alongside TRUE's, which is why the TRUE store's FAQ and returns pages mention Octane.
            TRUE is not part of iFIT, Johnson Health Tech or Dyaco, the groups behind most of the
            brands it competes with.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Where are they made?</h3>
          <p>
            TRUE's site describes the 1990 move to a larger manufacturing building in Missouri, but
            the current product pages we read do not say where today's treadmills are built. The one
            official statement we found is the CPSC's 2023 recall notice, which lists TRUE as the
            importer of consoles manufactured in China. That covers one console, not every treadmill,
            so treat "American company" and "American-made" as different claims, and ask the dealer
            where the frame and electronics of the model you are quoted are made if that matters to
            you.
          </p>
          <p>
            One naming trap: TRUE Fitness is also the name of an unrelated gym chain in Singapore and
            Malaysia. Searches for "true fitness" mix the two, so check you are on truefitness.com or
            shop.truefitness.com before reading prices or warranty terms.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Current TRUE Home Treadmill Range</h2>
<p>
            On 20 October 2026 TRUE's home store listed four treadmills, none marked sold out. The
            table gives the figures on which TRUE and an independent retailer agree, with the price at
            TRUE and at The Fitness Outlet on that day.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[560px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Running surface</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Deck and belt</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Parts cover</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">At TRUE</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">At The Fitness Outlet</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Z500</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 54 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FITX, standard belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,499</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,199</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Performance 1000</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">21 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FITX, standard belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 or 7 years (sources differ)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$5,199-$6,199</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,199</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Performance 3000</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">21 x 60 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">FITX, orthopedic belt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$5,499-$6,499</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,499</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Performance 8000</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">60 in. long; 21 or 22 in. wide (sources differ)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Soft Select and FITX, orthopedic belt, reversible deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">10 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$5,999-$6,999</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$4,999</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            All four share a 3 HP motor rating, a speed range of 0.5 to 12 mph, incline from 0 to 15%,
            a 350 lb maximum user weight, a 120-volt, 15-amp supply, a 25-year frame warranty and one
            year of labour. None has decline, and TRUE does not describe any of them as folding.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Consoles, and why the prices differ</h3>
          <p>
            The Z500 comes with an 8.5-inch LCD. The Performance models are sold with a choice of
            console, and this is where most of the price spread comes from. TRUE's store offered the
            T10, T16 and T22 touchscreens, at 10, 16 and 22 inches, with a $1,000 range between the
            cheapest and dearest configuration of each model. The Fitness Outlet also listed an 8.5-inch
            LCD option, and its lower prices may reflect that, though its pages did not say which
            console each price includes. TRUE's 2025 spec sheets, still hosted by Abt, describe a
            different pair of consoles, a 9-inch touchscreen and the 8.5-inch LCD, so the console
            line-up has changed recently. Abt also listed a Performance 8000 base at $4,600 with no
            console at all.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical point is that a TRUE price means nothing until you know the console, the
            delivery level and who assembles it. Ask for a quote that names all three.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-model" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Z500 or Performance 1000, 3000 or 8000: Which One?</h2>
<p>
            Because the three Performance models share a motor rating, speed range, incline range and
            user limit, the choice is about the running surface, the deck feel and the warranty, not
            about power.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Z500 if you walk</h3>
          <p>
            The Z500's 54-inch running surface is its defining number. For walking, incline walking and
            easy jogging it is enough, and its 72-inch length fits rooms where the Performance models'
            79 inches will not. For running, it is short: most guidance puts a running deck at 60
            inches, because a full stride needs room in front and behind. If anyone in the house runs,
            skip it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The Performance 1000 for most runners</h3>
          <p>
            The 1000 has the full 60-inch deck, HRC Cruise Control with a strap, and 25 years on the
            frame and motor. It is the cheapest way to get everything that matters about a TRUE, and on
            the day we checked it was $300 less than the 3000 at TRUE's prices, and $1,000 less again at
            one specialist retailer.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 3000 for the softer belt</h3>
          <p>
            The 3000 changes the belt to TRUE's orthopedic belt and lengthens parts cover to seven years
            on TRUE's page. If your joints prefer a softer surface and you value the extra parts cover,
            it is a modest step up.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 8000 for an adjustable deck</h3>
          <p>
            The 8000 adds Soft Select, a deck you can set softer or firmer, a reversible deck and ten
            years of parts cover. It is the one to buy if two people with different preferences share
            the machine, or if you want the longest warranty TRUE offers at home. Do not buy it
            expecting more speed or strength: it has the same 3 HP rating and 12 mph top speed as the
            1000. On that rating, TRUE footnotes every horsepower figure as a laboratory dynamometer
            result that does not reflect working horsepower at home; our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why the motor warranty is the better signal.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-hrc" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Soft System, FITX, Soft Select and HRC Explained</h2>
<p>
            TRUE's reputation among home buyers rests on two features it has sold for decades: a
            cushioned deck and heart-rate control. Both have been renamed over the years, which
            confuses anyone comparing a new TRUE with a used one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">From Soft System to FITX</h3>
          <p>
            TRUE's timeline dates its patented Soft System shock absorption to 1992, and older home
            treadmills, such as the M30 and Performance 300, were sold on it. TRUE's November 2023 blog
            post introducing the Performance Series still described the 1000 with Soft System cushioning; the current product
            pages and spec sheets call the cushioning the FITX, or Foot Impact Transfer, system. TRUE
            describes FITX as neoprene shock absorbers at the front of the deck, where the foot lands,
            with a firmer rear for push-off. Whether FITX is a rename or a redesign of Soft System, TRUE
            does not say; the principle described is the same, a softer landing zone and a firmer
            take-off zone.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Soft Select</h3>
          <p>
            Soft Select, which TRUE dates to 1998, is an adjustable version, and in the current home
            range only the Performance 8000 has it. TRUE says one adjustment moves the deck from soft,
            like a track, to firm, like pavement. Abt's listing and The Fitness Outlet's agree that the
            8000 has it. A softer deck is a matter of comfort and preference; the evidence that deck
            cushioning prevents injury is not strong, so buy it because you like how it feels, not as a
            treatment.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">HRC and HRC Cruise Control</h3>
          <p>
            TRUE patented HRC, its heart-rate control, in 1996 and introduced HRC Cruise Control in
            2007. On the current Performance models you set a target heart rate and the treadmill
            adjusts speed and incline to hold it, and TRUE's spec sheets say you can switch it on at any
            point in a workout. All three Performance models come with a Polar heart-rate strap; the
            Z500 has contact grips and lists target heart-rate workouts, and works with a Polar strap,
            but TRUE says one is not included. Use a chest strap for heart-rate
            control. Hand grips read the pulse only while you hold them, which you cannot do while
            running, and a heart-rate programme driven by intermittent readings will steer erratically.
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-600">
            <strong>Scope.</strong> We are a treadmill equipment site, not clinicians, and this is
            equipment advice, not medical advice. A treadmill's heart-rate control holds the number you
            give it; it does not know whether that number is right for you. If you have a heart
            condition, take medication that affects heart rate, or have been given a heart-rate limit,
            ask your doctor what target to use before relying on an automatic programme.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. The TRUE Warranty, Read Properly</h2>
<p>
            TRUE's current residential terms, from its product pages and agreed by the retailers we
            checked, are 25 years on the frame for every home treadmill, 25 years on the motor for the
            Performance models and 15 for the Z500, parts cover of five to ten years depending on model,
            three years on the Performance consoles and five on the Z500's, and one year of labour.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two points stand out. First, these are shorter than the terms many buyers remember: older
            TRUE home treadmills were sold with a lifetime frame and, on some models, a 30-year motor,
            and Treadmill Review Guru's TRUE coverage and older retailer listings still show those
            figures. One retailer's comparison table even gave the Performance 8000 a lifetime frame and
            motor while the warranty section on the same page said 25 years. Current terms are the 25-year
            ones. Second, the 1000's parts cover is five years on TRUE's page and seven on its 2025 spec
            sheet, so confirm it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The conditions that matter</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Professional first assembly.</strong> TRUE's warranty page says the warranty is valid only if the product is first assembled or installed by a TRUE authorised dealer or technician, unless TRUE authorises otherwise in writing. Its shipping page warns that self-assembly after curbside delivery may void the warranty.</li>
            <li><strong>Register within 30 days.</strong> Registration is online, or by a mailed form signed by the original purchaser and sent within 30 days of receipt, with the serial number intact.</li>
            <li><strong>Original purchaser only.</strong> The limited warranty applies only while the treadmill stays with the original purchaser. It does not transfer.</li>
            <li><strong>Home use.</strong> TRUE's FAQ says residential units are for home use only.</li>
            <li><strong>Maintenance.</strong> TRUE's FAQ recommends quarterly belt and deck lubrication and only its own Silax lubricant to keep the warranty in effect.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Service comes from the authorised dealer you bought from, which TRUE says will carry out
            warranty work at no cost, or from TRUE directly if you bought from its store. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            covers the general questions to ask any seller, and they apply with extra force here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Dealer-Only Buying: What It Means for Price and Service</h2>
<p>
            TRUE sells home treadmills in two ways: through its own online store, and through
            authorised specialist dealers found with the locator on truefitness.com. You will not find
            the current range in general big-box stores, and we could not confirm a new TRUE home
            treadmill sold on Amazon when we checked, so treat any Amazon search as a starting point
            rather than a source.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Price</h3>
          <p>
            TRUE's own store is not the cheapest place to buy a TRUE. On 20 October 2026 The Fitness
            Outlet, which describes itself as a long-standing TRUE dealer, showed every model below
            TRUE's lowest price, by $300 on the Z500 and by $1,000 on each Performance model. TRUE's
            FAQ also says it will not refund the difference if a price falls after you buy. Dealers
            have room to negotiate, run sales, and bundle delivery or a mat; TRUE's store mostly does
            not. Get two dealer quotes as well as TRUE's price, and compare them on the same console.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delivery and installation</h3>
          <p>
            TRUE's store offers three delivery levels: collection from St. Charles by appointment,
            curbside delivery to the end of the drive, or delivery and installation by an authorised
            TRUE installer, which TRUE recommends and says usually takes one to two hours. It quotes about
            two weeks for delivery after three or four days of processing, ships only to the contiguous
            US, and notes that some products are limited in some areas by retail-partner agreements.
            We could not see the cost of each level on its shipping page; get it in writing. Abt, a large
            retailer, sold the 8000 base by phone or in store only and described its scheduled delivery
            as excluding unpacking and set-up. Given the first-assembly condition in TRUE's warranty,
            curbside delivery is a false economy unless TRUE confirms in writing that your assembly
            is acceptable.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Service</h3>
          <p>
            The dealer model's real advantage is after the sale. A local dealer that installs the
            machine is usually the one that services it, and TRUE says authorised dealers carry out
            warranty work free. Before buying from a dealer far away for a lower price, ask who will
            come out when the belt or a board fails, and how long that usually takes where you live.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns</h3>
          <p>
            Returns to TRUE's store must be in new condition and original packaging within 30 days of
            the invoice, with an authorisation number, and you pay return shipping on shipped orders
            and lose the shipping fee. A 300 lb treadmill that has been assembled is not in its
            original packaging, so in practice the return window is narrow. Try one at a dealer
            first.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="commercial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. TRUE Commercial Treadmills, and Putting One at Home</h2>
<p>
            Most TRUE treadmills are not sold for the home. In 2024 TRUE reorganised its commercial
            cardio into four series, and on 20 October 2026 its site listed six treadmills across
            them: the Stryker Slat Treadmill and Alpine Runner incline trainer in the top Vapor
            Series, the Apex Treadmill in the Apex Series for heavy-use facilities, the Gravity and
            Gravity Plus in the Gravity Series, and the compact Launch Treadmill for facilities with
            limited space and budgets. These are the machines you meet in clubs, colleges and
            apartment gyms, which is why TRUE appears in our guide to
            <a href="/what-treadmills-do-gyms-use/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">what treadmills gyms use</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Apex shows the step up from the home range. TRUE's page and Schellers' listing agree on
            a 22 by 60-inch running surface, a 5 HP continuous-duty AC motor, 0.5 to 17 mph, incline from
            -3% to 18% with the decline kit, and a 500 lb user rating, with a warranty of seven years on
            the frame, three on parts and two of labour. Schellers listed it at $10,699 to $13,899 and as
            currently unavailable. TRUE adds that it needs a dedicated 120-volt, 20-amp circuit, which
            most home treadmill outlets are not. TRUE also lists the Stryker Slat at 0.5 to 12.5 mph and
            up to 25% incline with a 500 lb user rating, figures we did not see confirmed elsewhere.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Should you buy a commercial TRUE for home?</h3>
          <p>
            Only with the paperwork straight. Commercial machines carry commercial warranties written
            for facilities, often with shorter terms on parts than the home range, and TRUE's FAQ says
            only that it may offer extended warranties for commercial cardio used in a retail setting.
            Ask the dealer, in writing, what warranty applies to a commercial TRUE in a private home.
            Also plan the power: the Apex's 20-amp requirement means an electrician for many buyers. For
            most home users, a Performance model gives the TRUE build at half the price, with a
            warranty written for the way you will use it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The 2023 console recall</h3>
          <p>
            In July 2023 the CPSC announced a TRUE recall of about 600 Showrunner II consoles, fitted
            to equipment including the TC900 treadmill, because part of the wireless charging board
            could touch internal wiring and create a fire hazard. They were sold to clubs and gyms, and
            through specialty stores and distributors, from September 2021 to September 2022. TRUE
            received one report of a fire and none of injury, and the remedy was a free replacement
            console. It did not involve the current home range, but it matters if you are offered an
            ex-club TC900.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="true-vs-rivals" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. TRUE vs Sole, Spirit and Landice</h2>
<p>
            TRUE shares specialist showrooms with Spirit and Landice, and buyers often arrive having
            priced a Sole online. The four take different approaches to the same buyer.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Deck</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Frame and motor cover</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Maker's price</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">TRUE Performance 1000</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">21 x 60 in., non-folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">25 years / 25 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$5,199-$6,199</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sole F80</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 60 in., folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime / lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$1,499.99 (sale)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Spirit XT685</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">22 x 60 in., non-folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime / lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,199.99</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Landice L7</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">20 x 58 in., non-folding</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime parts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">From $5,429</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Prices are each maker's own on 20 October 2026; deck and warranty figures are from each
            maker's current page, as cross-checked in our brand guides.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">TRUE vs Sole</h3>
          <p>
            Sole sells direct online at a fraction of TRUE's price, and its F80 folds and carries
            lifetime frame and motor cover. A TRUE is heavier, does not fold, comes with a heart-rate
            strap and automatic heart-rate control, and is installed by a dealer. If price and a folding
            frame lead, Sole wins easily. If you want dealer installation and service and will use
            heart-rate training, the TRUE case is stronger, but it is a case for paying several times as
            much.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">TRUE vs Spirit</h3>
          <p>
            Spirit is the closest like-for-like rival: dealer-sold, non-folding XT685, 22 by 60-inch
            deck, lifetime frame, motor and deck cover with ten years on parts, at about $2,000 less than
            the cheapest Performance 1000 at TRUE's price. On the published warranty and the deck,
            Spirit gives more for less. The TRUE advantages are heart-rate control with a strap in the
            box and, on the 8000, an adjustable deck. Our
            <a href="/spirit-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Spirit treadmill guide</a>
            covers the XT range in detail.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">TRUE vs Landice</h3>
          <p>
            Landice is the other family-owned, dealer-sold American maker, and the price is similar. The
            L7 offers lifetime parts cover against TRUE's five to ten years, but a shorter 58-inch running
            length, and Landice ties its labour cover to installation by a nearby dealer, much as TRUE
            ties its warranty to authorised first assembly. Choose on deck length, console and which
            brand has the better dealer near you; our
            <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Landice guide</a>
            sets out its range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-true" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Buying a Used TRUE Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            TRUE treadmills are common on the used market, partly because so many come out of clubs,
            hotels and apartment gyms when they refit. A well-kept used TRUE can be a good buy. It also
            comes with no warranty at all, because TRUE's cover belongs to the original purchaser and
            does not transfer.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Names you will see</h3>
          <p>
            Older home models include the M30 and M50, the Z5.0 and Z5.4, the Performance 100, 300 and
            800, the 950, and the Excel 900, which a 2017 TRUE release said was previously known as the ES900.
            Commercial units include 650-series machines sold as the TC650, C650 and CS650, and the
            TC900. We could
            not confirm that any of these is still sold new; TRUE's current commercial pages list the
            six treadmills above. The model and serial number on the frame label let a dealer or TRUE's
            parts and service line decode the year.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Check the recalls</h3>
          <p>
            Two CPSC recalls touch TRUE treadmills. The 2023 Showrunner II console recall covers the
            TC900 commercial treadmill; look for a product number beginning C4P on the label on the rear
            console cover and ask whether the console was replaced. The 1998 Pacific Scientific recall
            covered motors in TRUE's 350, 450, 500, 700 and 725 series sold from July 1997 to October
            1998, with an electrical short that posed shock and fire hazards. Those machines are now
            nearly 30 years old, but any very old TRUE should be checked against the CPSC database before it is plugged in.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What to inspect</h3>
          <p>
            Walk and run on it at your normal speed for several minutes. Listen for the motor labouring
            when you land, watch for the belt hesitating, run the incline through its full range, and
            test the heart-rate control with a strap if that is why you want a TRUE. Check the deck
            under the belt for wear, and ask whether a cushioned Soft System or FITX deck has had its
            absorbers replaced. Price a technician's visit before you pay, because there is no warranty
            to fall back on. Our
            <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            gives the full inspection, and the power and access questions for an ex-club unit are the
            same as for any commercial machine moved into a home.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who makes TRUE treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                TRUE Fitness Technology, a privately held, family-owned company in the St. Louis area of Missouri, founded by Frank Trulaske. Its site gives its headquarters as St. Charles; older releases say O’Fallon. TRUE dates its start to 1979 as TRUE Sports, bought Octane Fitness in 2020, and is not part of iFIT, Johnson Health Tech or Dyaco.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a TRUE treadmill cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 20 October 2026 TRUE’s store listed the Z500 at $3,499, the Performance 1000 at $5,199 to $6,199, the 3000 at $5,499 to $6,499 and the 8000 at $5,999 to $6,999, depending on console. A specialist dealer showed each model for $300 to $1,000 less the same day. Prices change, so compare quotes on the same console and delivery terms.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between the TRUE Performance 1000, 3000 and 8000?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They share a 60-inch running length, a 3 HP rating, 12 mph, 15% incline and a 350 lb limit. The 3000 adds an orthopedic belt and longer parts cover. The 8000 adds the Soft Select adjustable deck, a reversible deck and ten years of parts cover. None is more powerful than the others.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What warranty comes with a TRUE treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Current home terms are 25 years on the frame, 25 years on the motor for the Performance models and 15 for the Z500, five to ten years on parts, and one year of labour. TRUE says the warranty is valid only after first assembly by an authorised dealer or technician, must be registered within 30 days, and does not transfer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is TRUE HRC?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                HRC is TRUE’s heart-rate control, which it patented in 1996. With HRC Cruise Control you set a target heart rate and the treadmill adjusts speed and incline to hold it. The Performance models include a Polar strap, which gives far steadier readings than hand grips. Ask your doctor what target to use if you have a heart condition.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I buy a TRUE treadmill on Amazon?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                We could not confirm a new TRUE home treadmill sold on Amazon when we checked. TRUE sells through its own store and authorised dealers, and buying from one matters, because TRUE’s warranty depends on authorised first assembly and dealers carry out warranty service.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a TRUE Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">TRUE treadmills are solid machines sold in a way that catches buyers out. These are the errors that cost money or warranty cover.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing curbside delivery and assembling it yourself</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">TRUE’s warranty is valid only if an authorised dealer or technician does the first assembly, unless TRUE agrees otherwise in writing, and its shipping page warns that self-assembly may void it. Saving the installation fee can cost the whole warranty. Pay for delivery and installation, or get TRUE’s written authorisation before you open the box.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying for the 8000 expecting a stronger motor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The Performance 1000, 3000 and 8000 share a 3 HP rating, a 12 mph top speed, 15% incline and a 350 lb limit. The extra money buys the deck, the belt and longer parts cover. If you want power or speed, none of them changes; if you want the deck feel, walk on each at a dealer before choosing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing prices without naming the console</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Each Performance model spans $1,000 at TRUE depending on console, some retailers list an LCD option TRUE’s store does not, and Abt sold an 8000 base with no console at all. A cheaper quote may simply be a smaller screen. Ask every seller for the console, delivery level and installer in writing, then compare like with like.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a used TRUE as if the warranty came with it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">TRUE’s warranty belongs to the original purchaser and does not transfer, so a two-year-old TRUE has no cover however long the frame term was. Ask for the model and serial number, check it against the CPSC recalls, run it at your own speed, and price a technician’s visit before agreeing a figure.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

